# Mutation cases for "a narrow broadcast log reads its head modulo the stamp space".
#
# Run with
# `scripts/mutate sce-build/tests/mutations/a_narrow_broadcast_log_reads_its_head_modulo_the_stamp_space.cases`.
#
# The narrow row of the bounded broadcast log (ADR 0062, "Stamp width") keeps a stamp
# narrower than 64 bits, so its count wraps. It is the one implementation of the
# 64-bit row (`broadcast_log_core.rs`) over a stamp of `w` bits, and what makes it
# right across the wrap is arithmetic: the head a reader loads is a count modulo
# `2^w` and the reader takes its distance from its own cursor modulo `2^w`; the
# cursor and the sequence numbers are counts modulo `2^w`; the stamp is the count
# truncated to `w` bits; and the log tells a young log from one that has wrapped by a
# stamp no protocol write equals. The memory orders are the other casefile's
# (`a_broadcast_log_orders_every_slot_hand_over.cases`), which the 64-bit models
# kill; the decisions here are the ones the 64-bit models cannot see, because a
# 64-bit count never wraps.
#
# The oracle is the loom models over a stamp of THREE bits
# (`backends/rust/forge-runtime/tests/loom_broadcast_log_narrow.rs`): a 32-bit stamp
# does not wrap in a model, so a mutant of the wrap would pass against one. The
# models start from a log that has already published six or seven entries, so a few
# more cross the head's wrap (at 8) and the stamps' repeat (at 4 entries), and they
# hold every answer to the exact specification through a count of what the publisher
# has published.
#
# Cases that are not observable by these models are not claimed; each has its
# reason where it would stand:
#
# - The slot-count check of `BroadcastLog::new` (a power of two, at most half the
#   stamp space). It is a compile-time refusal, and the models use a size it
#   accepts, so removing it changes no run. Its oracle is the TLA+ configuration
#   `docs/formal/BroadcastLogWrapped_non_divisor.cfg`, in which TLC finds the
#   reader of a log whose slot count does not divide the stamp space reading the
#   wrong slot after the wrap (`scripts/formal/check.sh`).
# - The slot index itself. For a slot count that divides the stamp space, the index
#   of a reader's cursor, of the same cursor a lap on, and of the publisher's count
#   are one number, so no change of the form "index from another lap" is a change
#   at all; only a slot count that does not divide breaks it, which the models
#   cannot build (the case above).
# - The lap added to a lag counted from a head that has wrapped
#   (`BroadcastLog::lagged`). It is reached only by a reader a whole stamp space
#   behind during a mismatch, outside the bound the models keep; the unit test
#   `a_head_that_wrapped_during_a_mismatch_still_gives_a_lag_of_at_least_one`
#   holds it.
# - The position a reader opened at a given sequence number takes
#   (`BroadcastLog::reader_at`). The models open at the head and at the oldest;
#   the unit test `a_position_is_read_modulo_the_stamp_space_and_one_ahead_is_the_head`
#   holds it.
#
# The models build only under `--cfg loom`; the cfg goes in as a cargo `--config`, as
# in the other casefile, and the build has the target directory of its own.
#
# Anchors are literal slices of the source; an edit that moves one is reported
# INCONCLUSIVE with the anchor quoted, never as a pass.

mutation_tests -p sce-forge-runtime --release --target-dir target/loom --config 'build.rustflags=["--cfg","loom"]' --test loom_broadcast_log_narrow
mutation_targets backends/rust/forge-runtime/src/broadcast_log_core.rs
mutation_oracles backends/rust/forge-runtime/tests/loom_broadcast_log_narrow.rs

# ── The head is extended against the cursor, modulo the stamp space ─────

# A distance taken without the reduction is a huge number as soon as the head's
# residue is below the cursor's, which is the first read after the head wraps.
mutation_case "a reader takes its distance from the head without reducing it to the stamp space" <<'PY'
edit("backends/rust/forge-runtime/src/broadcast_log_core.rs",
     r'''        head.wrapping_sub(cursor) & Self::MASK''',
     r'''        head.wrapping_sub(cursor)''')
PY

# A distance that stops at zero reads a head that has wrapped as no entries at all.
mutation_case "a reader reads a head below its cursor as the cursor's own place" <<'PY'
edit("backends/rust/forge-runtime/src/broadcast_log_core.rs",
     r'''        head.wrapping_sub(cursor) & Self::MASK''',
     r'''        head.saturating_sub(cursor) & Self::MASK''')
PY

# ── The cursor and the sequence numbers are counts modulo the stamp space ──

mutation_case "a reader moves its cursor past the stamp space without reducing it" <<'PY'
edit("backends/rust/forge-runtime/src/broadcast_log_core.rs",
     r'''        cursor.wrapping_add(by) & Self::MASK''',
     r'''        cursor.wrapping_add(by)''')
PY

# ── The stamp is the count truncated to the stamp's width ────────────────

# A stamp space of half the size reads every residue modulo 4 where the log's
# sequence numbers are modulo 8.
mutation_case "the stamp space is half the width the stamp has" <<'PY'
edit("backends/rust/forge-runtime/src/broadcast_log_core.rs",
     r'''        (1u64 << L::BITS) - 1''',
     r'''        (1u64 << (L::BITS - 1)) - 1''')
PY

# A done stamp that keeps the bit above the stamp is a wider stamp than the log
# declares: it does not repeat after half the space, so a reader held for that long is
# never shown a later lap as its own. A safer log is not a fault the positive models
# can show; the one model that notices is the one that holds a reader outside the
# bound and requires it to be fooled (`outside_the_bound_a_later_lap_is_mistaken_for_
# the_readers_own`), which then passes without a panic and fails. That the bound is
# needed, and that it is the stamp's truncation that makes it so, is what this case
# keeps.
mutation_case "the done stamp is not truncated to the stamp's width" <<'PY'
edit("backends/rust/forge-runtime/src/broadcast_log_core.rs",
     r'''        n.wrapping_mul(2).wrapping_add(2) & Self::MASK''',
     r'''        n.wrapping_mul(2).wrapping_add(2)''')
PY

# ── A young log is told from one that wrapped by the last slot's stamp ──────

# Read the other way, a log that has published nothing is a log that has wrapped, and
# the reader opened at the oldest is put R behind a head of 0.
mutation_case "the oldest reader takes a written last slot for an unwritten one" <<'PY'
edit("backends/rust/forge-runtime/src/broadcast_log_core.rs",
     r'''== L::UNWRITTEN;''',
     r'''!= L::UNWRITTEN;''')
PY

# Unwritten as 0 is a stamp the protocol writes: done(n) of the entry 3 mod 4 is 0
# over a stamp of three bits. The model that opens a reader at the oldest while the
# publisher writes the seventh entry into the last slot sees it read as unwritten.
mutation_case "an unwritten slot's stamp is one the protocol writes into the last slot" <<'PY'
edit("backends/rust/forge-runtime/src/broadcast_log_core.rs",
     r'''    const UNWRITTEN: u64 = 1;''',
     r'''    const UNWRITTEN: u64 = 0;''')
PY

# A reader opened at the oldest of a log that has wrapped is RETENTION behind the head,
# modulo the stamp space.
mutation_case "the oldest reader of a log that has wrapped is placed at the head" <<'PY'
edit("backends/rust/forge-runtime/src/broadcast_log_core.rs",
     r'''            Self::distance(self.head(), Self::RETENTION)''',
     r'''            self.head()''')
PY
